      "text": "We start with the given equation \\(\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}\\). Recall that \\(\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\) and \\(\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the given equation as:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{1 + \\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}.\n\\]\nNext, we cross-multiply to obtain:\n\\[\n3(1 + \\sin x) = 4 \\cos x.\n\\]\nWe can express \\(\\cos x\\) in terms of \\(\\sin x\\) using the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos x = \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 x}\\) (since \\(\\sec x > 0\\), we will consider the positive root for simplicity):\n\\[\n3(1 + \\sin x) = 4 \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 x}.\n\\]\nTo eliminate the square root, we square both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n9(1 + \\sin x)^2 = 16(1 - \\sin^2 x).\n\\]\nExpanding both sides, we get:\n\\[\n9(1 + 2\\sin x + \\sin^2 x) = 16 - 16\\sin^2 x.\n\\]\nSimplifying further, we have:\n\\[\n9 + 18\\sin x + 9\\sin^2 x = 16 - 16\\sin^2 x.\n\\]\nRearranging terms, we obtain:\n\\[\n25\\sin^2 x + 18\\sin x - 7 = 0.\n\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(\\sin x\\). We solve it using the quadratic formula \\(\\sin x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 25\\), \\(b = 18\\), and \\(c = -7\\):\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 \\pm \\sqrt{18^2 - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot (-7)}}{2 \\cdot 25} = \\frac{-18 \\pm \\sqrt{324 + 700}}{50} = \\frac{-18 \\pm \\sqrt{1024}}{50} = \\frac{-18 \\pm 32}{50}.\n\\]\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{14}{50} = \\frac{7}{25} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sin x = \\frac{-50}{50} = -1.\n\\]\nThus, the possible values of \\(\\sin x\\) are \\(\\boxed{\\left\\{\\frac{7}{25}, -1\\right\\}}\\).",
