      "text": "To solve the given equation \\(\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}\\), we start by expressing \\(\\sec x\\) and \\(\\tan x\\) in terms of \\(\\sin x\\) and \\(\\cos x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n\\]\n\nThus, the given equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\nCombining the terms on the left-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 + \\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n1 + \\sin x = \\frac{4}{3} \\cos x\n\\]\n\nWe can rearrange this equation to isolate \\(\\cos x\\):\n\n\\[\n3(1 + \\sin x) = 4 \\cos x\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2 x = 1 - \\sin^2 x\\) to express \\(\\cos x\\) in terms of \\(\\sin x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\cos x = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 x}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n3(1 + \\sin x) = 4 \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 x}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the square root, we square both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n9(1 + \\sin x)^2 = 16(1 - \\sin^2 x)\n\\]\n\nExpanding both sides, we have:\n\n\\[\n9(1 + 2 \\sin x + \\sin^2 x) = 16 - 16 \\sin^2 x\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n9 + 18 \\sin x + 9 \\sin^2 x = 16 - 16 \\sin^2 x\n\\]\n\nRearranging all terms to one side, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n25 \\sin^2 x + 18 \\sin x - 7 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(\\sin x\\). We solve it using the quadratic formula \\( \\sin x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\(a = 25\\), \\(b = 18\\), and \\(c = -7\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 \\pm \\sqrt{18^2 - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot (-7)}}{2 \\cdot 25}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying inside the square root:\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 \\pm \\sqrt{324 + 700}}{50} = \\frac{-18 \\pm \\sqrt{1024}}{50} = \\frac{-18 \\pm 32}{50}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two potential solutions for \\(\\sin x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 + 32}{50} = \\frac{14}{50} = \\frac{7}{25}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 - 32}{50} = \\frac{-50}{