      "text": "Let the given equation be\n\\[\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}.\\]By dividing both sides by $\\cos x,$ we get\n\\[\\frac{1}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}.\\]That is, \\[\\frac{1 + \\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}.\\]Then $3(1 + \\sin x) = 4 \\cos x,$ so $3 + 3 \\sin x = 4 \\cos x.$\nWe square both sides to get \\[\\left(3 + 3 \\sin x\\right)^2 = \\left(4 \\cos x\\right)^2,\\]which expands to $9 + 18 \\sin x + 9 \\sin^2 x = 16 \\cos^2 x.$\nWe know that $\\sec^2 x - \\tan^2 x = 1,$ so $\\left(\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\right)^2 - \\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\right)^2 = 1,$\nor $\\frac{1 - \\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x} = 1.$\nWe can then multiply this equation by $\\cos^2 x$ to get $1 - \\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x.$\nSubstituting into the other equation, we have $9 + 18 \\sin x + 9 \\sin^2 x = 16 \\cos^2 x = 16 (1 - \\sin^2 x).$\nSimplifying, we get $25 \\sin^2 x + 18 \\sin x - 7 = 0.$\nThis quadratic equation can be factored as $(5 \\sin x + 7)(5 \\sin x - 1) = 0.$\nSetting each factor equal to zero, we find $\\sin x = -\\frac{7}{5}$ or $\\sin x = \\frac{1}{5}.$\nSince $\\sec x$ and $\\tan x$ are both positive, $\\sin x$ cannot be negative.\nTherefore, the only possible value of $\\sin x$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{5}}.$The answer is: \\frac{1}{5}",
