      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the given equation \\(\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}\\), we start by expressing \\(\\sec x\\) and \\(\\tan x\\) in terms of \\(\\sin x\\) and \\(\\cos x\\). Recall that \\(\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\) and \\(\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\). Therefore, the given equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe can combine the fractions on the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 + \\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n1 + \\sin x = \\frac{4}{3} \\cos x\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\). Let's express \\(\\cos x\\) in terms of \\(\\sin x\\). From the equation \\(1 + \\sin x = \\frac{4}{3} \\cos x\\), we solve for \\(\\cos x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\cos x = \\frac{3(1 + \\sin x)}{4}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute this expression for \\(\\cos x\\) into the Pythagorean identity:\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2 x + \\left(\\frac{3(1 + \\sin x)}{4}\\right)^2 = 1\n\\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2 x + \\frac{9(1 + \\sin x)^2}{16} = 1\n\\]\n\nMultiply through by 16 to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[\n16 \\sin^2 x + 9(1 + 2 \\sin x + \\sin^2 x) = 16\n\\]\n\nExpand and combine like terms:\n\n\\[\n16 \\sin^2 x + 9 + 18 \\sin x + 9 \\sin^2 x = 16\n\\]\n\n\\[\n25 \\sin^2 x + 18 \\sin x + 9 = 16\n\\]\n\nSubtract 16 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n25 \\sin^2 x + 18 \\sin x - 7 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in terms of \\(\\sin x\\). We solve it using the quadratic formula \\( \\sin x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\(a = 25\\), \\(b = 18\\), and \\(c = -7\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 \\pm \\sqrt{18^2 - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot (-7)}}{2 \\cdot 25}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 \\pm \\sqrt{324 + 700}}{50}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 \\pm \\sqrt{1024}}{50}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 \\pm 32}{50}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two possible solutions:\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{14}{50} = \\frac{7}{25}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-50}{50} = -1\n\\]\n\nHowever, \\(\\sin x = -1\\) is not possible because it would imply \\(\\cos x = 0\\) and the original equation would not hold. Therefore, the only valid solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{7}{25}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\sin x\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{7}{25}}\\).",
